  "response": "Silent data corruption refers to a computation producing an incorrect result with no error reported by the hardware, and that is exactly the category into which the episode falls: the miscomputation was a silent data corruption \u2014 in other words, the data was corrupted without any error being signalled by the machine. Against that picture of a failure that announces itself nowhere in the logs stands the protection offered by ECC memory, which corrects double-bit errors within a single DRAM word.\n\nBecause the corruption occurred with no signal from the machine, this indicates that the machine contained a defective hardware component. The program contained a latent software defect as well, so at least one of the two faults \u2014 a bad part in the machine or a dormant bug in the code \u2014 was responsible, and perhaps both were. Adjudication accordingly begins with repeatability, and here the record admits only two mutually exclusive possibilities: either the incorrect result never recurred on the original machine, or it recurred when the job was re-run on that same machine; one of these must be the case, and they cannot both be.\n\nRe-running the identical binary on a different machine became a standard step in isolating a hardware fault as a result of the 2021 paper titled \"Cores that don't count\", in which Google engineers documented defective CPU cores producing erroneous results. That the wrong answer returned when the job was re-run on the original machine confirms that defective CPU cores fail deterministically on every repetition of the same workload. A result that never returns on the original machine, by contrast, is evidence of nondeterminism internal to the program's own execution rather than of silicon that misbehaves identically each time.\n\nData races in multithreaded code yield results that vary between executions of the same program, and it is precisely that variability which gave rise to deterministic record-and-replay tools, which capture nondeterministic inputs so that a failing execution can be re-executed exactly. Checksums on intermediate results localize the stage of a computation at which corruption first appears. Taken together, these instruments convert an unreproducible wrong answer into a tractable object of investigation. The identical binary is exercised on a second socket and a third, so that a fault confined to one part of the fleet separates itself from one that travels with the code; the suspect execution is captured and replayed under fixed inputs, so that a defect in the program must reappear if it is there at all; and the arithmetic is audited stage by stage until the first divergence is pinned to a particular kernel or a particular device. Where replay reproduces the error with every input held constant, the weight of evidence falls on the program. Where the same binary and the same recorded inputs diverge only on one machine, and the vendor's errata or margin tests corroborate the finding, the weight falls on the part.",
